The command echo, the receive progress lines and the transient
SERcvFileNotFoundXFTP store error of the cancelled first attempt are
produced by independent parts of the client, so the order in which they
reach the terminal is not fixed: accept them in any order instead of
requiring the command echo first. These are console lines and carry no
timestamp, so <### (which compares the lines as printed) is the operator
that matches them; <##? strips a timestamp and errors on the first line
that has none. No assertion is dropped.

Index: tests/ChatTests/Files.hs
--- tests/ChatTests/Files.hs.orig
+++ tests/ChatTests/Files.hs
@@ -1262,9 +1262,13 @@ testXFTPCancelRcvRepeat =
       bob ##> "/fs 1"
       bob <## "receiving file 1 (testfile) not accepted yet, use /fr 1 to receive file"

-      bob ##> ("/fr 1 " <> tmpDir bob)
+      let frCmd = "/fr 1 " <> tmpDir bob
+      bob `send` frCmd
+      -- The command echo and the receive lines come from independent parts of
+      -- the client, so the command echo is not ordered against them.
       bob
-        <### [ ConsoleString $ "saving file 1 from alice to " <> testfile1,
+        <### [ ConsoleString frCmd,
+               ConsoleString $ "saving file 1 from alice to " <> testfile1,
                "started receiving file 1 (testfile) from alice"
              ]
       bob <## "completed receiving file 1 (testfile) from alice"
